WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.260
Hey, welcome to Planetarian Daily, episode 67 for May 23, 2026.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.160
Let's see what the latest research is telling us.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:14.120
Ordinary Wi-Fi signals can identify people with near-perfect accuracy even when phones

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:14.640
are off.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:20.860
German researchers showed that analyzing how radio waves bounce around a room allows recognition

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:21.780
of individuals.

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:27.300
The method works even when a person carries no device and has turned their phone off.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:33.080
It relies on passive monitoring of ordinary Wi-Fi infrastructure rather than active scanning.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:38.780
The approach creates a new form of environmental surveillance that does not require line-of-sight

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:40.320
or wearable technology.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:45.380
Future work will examine how room layout and movement patterns affect accuracy.

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:49.140
Now shifting to a very different area of research.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:55.320
Ordinary Wi-Fi infrastructure can serve as a sensor because of body-alters reflections

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:56.500
in distinctive ways.

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:02.160
The German demonstration works without any device on the person, and functions even

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:03.900
when phones are powered off.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:08.500
It turns everyday wireless infrastructure into a passive sensing system.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:14.380
This raises immediate questions about privacy in homes and offices that already contain

00:01:14.380 --> 00:01:14.920
rotors.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:19.680
What safeguards or detection methods might emerge as this capability spreads.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:22.720
Now here's something most people get wrong.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:28.200
Most people assume that switching a phone off or leaving it behind removes them from digital

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:28.720
tracking.

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:34.320
In reality, the surrounding radio environment can still register a person's presence and

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.620
identity through passive signal analysis.

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:42.660
Right now, as you move through a room with a working Wi-Fi router, your body alters how

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:45.680
those waves reflect and scatter in measurable ways.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:51.060
Researchers have shown these alterations are distinctive enough for reliable recognition.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:57.040
A memorable detail is that identification succeeds even without any electronic signature

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:58.040
from the individual.

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:04.500
The practical takeaway is to treat wireless networks themselves as potential sensors and

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:08.740
to consider physical shielding or signal management strategies when privacy matters.

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:15.400
Next up, more than 60 years of records from Namibia and South Africa's West Coast show

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:18.560
